            <ArticleTitle>Clinics in diagnostic imaging (177). Medical meniscus bucket-handle tear with medial oblique meniscomeniscal ligament.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>241-245</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.11622/smedj.2017038</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 29-year-old man with a previous football injury to his left knee presented with pain of the same knee. The patient twisted it as he was turning a corner quickly while going up the stairs, leading to internal rotation of his femur on his tibia with his knee in flexion. MR imaging revealed a bucket-handle tear of the medial meniscus, as well as a complete tear of the anterior cruciate ligament. However, image interpretation was complicated by the presence of a medial oblique meniscomeniscal ligament, a rare normal variant among intermeniscal ligaments of the knee. All four recognised variants of intermeniscal ligaments are discussed, with emphasis on their prevalence, imaging and anatomical features, and the way in which they may mimic meniscal tears.</AbstractText>
